1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,150
Blakang Mati Before Sentosa: The
Island's Kampongs and Barracks

2
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:09,967
For most of its recorded history,
the island we now call Sentosa

3
00:00:10,047 --> 00:00:14,634
was Pulau Blakang Mati, a working
island of fishing villages,

4
00:00:14,714 --> 00:00:19,075
fruit gardens and, from 1878,
British forts and barracks.

5
00:00:19,075 --> 00:00:25,167
By 1959 about 1,000 people lived in
two kampongs on its northern shore,

6
00:00:25,247 --> 00:00:28,550
many of them working for the soldiers.

7
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:31,846
In 1970 the island was renamed Sentosa and

8
00:00:31,926 --> 00:00:35,623
rebuilt as a resort, and
its villagers moved to

9
00:00:35,703 --> 00:00:39,400
new flats across the
harbour at Telok Blangah.

10
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:41,600
The name is old.

11
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:45,158
The Portuguese mapmaker
Manuel Godinho de Erédia,

12
00:00:45,238 --> 00:00:50,133
who was born in Malacca, marked an
island called "Blacanmati" south

13
00:00:50,213 --> 00:00:54,662
of Singapore on his map of 1604,
and later European maps gave

14
00:00:54,742 --> 00:00:58,900
it other names, among them
Pulau Panjang, "long island".

15
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:02,933
In Malay, Blakang Mati is
usually translated as "behind

16
00:01:03,013 --> 00:01:07,500
death" or "dead back", and
nobody is sure how it came about.

17
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:10,503
One story ties it to
the pirates who preyed

18
00:01:10,583 --> 00:01:13,450
on ships in the narrow strait beside it.

19
00:01:13,450 --> 00:01:15,969
Another says that islanders who died

20
00:01:16,049 --> 00:01:18,575
fighting pirates were buried there.

21
00:01:18,575 --> 00:01:21,467
The island sat beside
the main passage into

22
00:01:21,547 --> 00:01:25,061
what became Keppel Harbour,
and ships often anchored

23
00:01:25,141 --> 00:01:27,975
off its western tip to wait for the tide.

24
00:01:27,975 --> 00:01:31,693
In the early 1830s a
visiting naturalist found

25
00:01:31,773 --> 00:01:34,748
neat Malay houses near a sandy beach,

26
00:01:34,828 --> 00:01:39,700
coconut palms and small
plantations of sugar cane and yams.

27
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:44,848
In 1833 the British put up a flag
signal station on Mount Serapong

28
00:01:44,928 --> 00:01:49,838
to warn the town of approaching
ships, but so many of its staff

29
00:01:49,918 --> 00:01:54,750
fell ill with fever that it was
moved to Mount Faber in 1845.

30
00:01:54,750 --> 00:01:59,450
In 1848 a British doctor, Robert
Little, published an account

31
00:01:59,530 --> 00:02:04,075
of the island after visiting
it to investigate that fever.

32
00:02:04,075 --> 00:02:07,847
He described three villages,
one of Bugis settlers,

33
00:02:07,927 --> 00:02:11,175
one of Malays and one
of Bugis and Chinese.

34
00:02:11,175 --> 00:02:15,434
He estimated that pineapples
covered at least 200 acres,

35
00:02:15,514 --> 00:02:19,000
and he called Blakang
Mati "one vast pinery".

36
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,195
The Bugis, he wrote, earned
more than the average

37
00:02:22,275 --> 00:02:25,002
in Singapore from their fruit and fishing,

38
00:02:25,082 --> 00:02:28,625
and their women wove sarongs
of silk and gold thread.

39
00:02:28,625 --> 00:02:32,660
As early as 1827 a British
officer warned that an

40
00:02:32,740 --> 00:02:37,275
enemy who took Blakang Mati
would command the harbour.

41
00:02:37,275 --> 00:02:41,170
Arguments over strategy and
cost delayed the defences

42
00:02:41,250 --> 00:02:44,995
for half a century, until
work began in 1878 on gun

43
00:02:45,075 --> 00:02:48,745
batteries at Mount Siloso,
in the west, and at the

44
00:02:48,825 --> 00:02:52,875
eastern end of the island,
later named Fort Connaught.

45
00:02:52,875 --> 00:02:56,375
Forts at Serapong and Imbiah followed.

46
00:02:56,375 --> 00:03:00,174
By 1887 the colonial government
had bought out all the

47
00:03:00,254 --> 00:03:04,628
private land on the island, which
may have meant clearing some

48
00:03:04,708 --> 00:03:08,507
of the old villages, but in
the 1890s it began issuing

49
00:03:08,587 --> 00:03:12,825
leases again for villages,
fishing grounds and plantations.

50
00:03:12,825 --> 00:03:17,475
The Fort Palmer post tells the
story of the guns themselves.

51
00:03:17,475 --> 00:03:20,039
Around the forts grew a garrison town:

52
00:03:20,119 --> 00:03:24,424
barracks blocks around a parade
ground, bungalows for officers,

53
00:03:24,504 --> 00:03:27,834
an Officers' Mess on a
hill, a military hospital,

54
00:03:27,914 --> 00:03:31,950
cookhouses, canteens, sea-bathing
enclosures and a cinema.

55
00:03:31,950 --> 00:03:35,350
Life there followed the colonial order.

56
00:03:35,350 --> 00:03:38,996
The Indian gunners of the
Hong Kong and Singapore Royal

57
00:03:39,076 --> 00:03:43,603
Artillery had separate quarters and
lower pay than British soldiers,

58
00:03:43,683 --> 00:03:46,800
and spent their free
time in different places.

59
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:48,988
Local people did much of the work:

60
00:03:49,068 --> 00:03:54,458
Indian laundrymen washed uniforms, Chinese
cooks and waiters ran the dining rooms,

61
00:03:54,538 --> 00:03:56,793
and a store in the village near the

62
00:03:56,873 --> 00:04:00,475
landing sold butter, tinned
milk and sweets on credit.

63
00:04:00,475 --> 00:04:04,350
A sampan to town cost 10 or 20 cents.

64
00:04:04,350 --> 00:04:08,345
When war came to Singapore,
Blakang Mati had the largest

65
00:04:08,425 --> 00:04:13,300
concentration of guns in the fire
command that guarded the harbour.

66
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:18,400
About 100 bombs fell on the
island on the 18th of January.

67
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:21,610
From the 11th of February
its guns fired inland

68
00:04:21,690 --> 00:04:23,990
at Japanese positions in the west,

69
00:04:24,070 --> 00:04:28,200
and Fort Connaught shelled the
captured airfield at Tengah.

70
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:32,906
Early on the 14th of February, with
false reports of Japanese landings

71
00:04:32,986 --> 00:04:36,257
coming in through the
smoke of burning oil tanks,

72
00:04:36,337 --> 00:04:41,875
Fort Siloso's guns were blown up, and
Connaught's followed about two hours later.

73
00:04:41,875 --> 00:04:44,875
Singapore surrendered the next day.

74
00:04:44,875 --> 00:04:48,812
The Japanese renamed the
island Kashima, "deer island",

75
00:04:48,892 --> 00:04:54,150
and an air force supply unit stored
its bombs in the island's magazines.

76
00:04:54,150 --> 00:04:58,932
Between 400 and 1,000 Allied
prisoners of war were held in two

77
00:04:59,012 --> 00:05:03,875
of its barrack blocks and made
to load and maintain the bombs.

78
00:05:03,875 --> 00:05:07,044
Days after the surrender,
prisoners on the island

79
00:05:07,124 --> 00:05:10,161
watched as Chinese
civilians, their hands tied,

80
00:05:10,241 --> 00:05:13,225
were pushed from boats
into the sea and shot.

81
00:05:13,225 --> 00:05:16,341
They were among the
victims of the Sook Ching,

82
00:05:16,421 --> 00:05:20,450
the Japanese military's purge
of the Chinese in Singapore.

83
00:05:20,450 --> 00:05:24,253
One officer later told a
war crimes trial that the

84
00:05:24,333 --> 00:05:27,981
prisoners recovered and
buried about 118 bodies,

85
00:05:28,061 --> 00:05:32,100
most of them workers of the
Singapore Harbour Board.

86
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:35,237
A historic site marker at Berhala Reping,

87
00:05:35,317 --> 00:05:39,475
now inside Sentosa's golf
club, records the killings.

88
00:05:39,475 --> 00:05:44,150
After the war the British
returned, and so did the islanders.

89
00:05:44,150 --> 00:05:47,619
In 1946 there were 377 Malay,

90
00:05:47,699 --> 00:05:51,125
Chinese and Tamil residents.

91
00:05:51,125 --> 00:05:53,837
By 1959 there were about 1,000,

92
00:05:53,917 --> 00:05:58,600
living mostly in two kampongs
on the northern shore.

93
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:02,088
The village by the jetty,
facing Keppel Harbour,

94
00:06:02,168 --> 00:06:06,850
was mainly Hokkien, and stood
where Universal Studios is today.

95
00:06:06,850 --> 00:06:11,983
Many of its families were Yeos from
Meishan village in Dongshan county,

96
00:06:12,063 --> 00:06:15,361
Fujian, who had settled
on the island early in

97
00:06:15,441 --> 00:06:19,700
the 20th century and spoke the
Zhao'an variety of Hokkien.

98
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:22,759
The village further west was mainly Malay,

99
00:06:22,839 --> 00:06:27,025
with a few Chinese families
who ran its provision shops.

100
00:06:27,025 --> 00:06:31,353
When the Prime Minister, Lee
Kuan Yew, visited in 1964,

101
00:06:31,433 --> 00:06:37,525
he noted that about three-quarters of
its people had roots in Riau and Java.

102
00:06:37,525 --> 00:06:41,270
Former residents remember
the two villages sending

103
00:06:41,350 --> 00:06:45,175
each other food at Chinese
New Year and Hari Raya.

104
00:06:45,175 --> 00:06:49,094
In the first half of the
century most islanders who did

105
00:06:49,174 --> 00:06:53,100
not work for the army fished
or ran small ferry boats.

106
00:06:53,100 --> 00:06:56,300
By the 1950s fishing was mostly a pastime,

107
00:06:56,380 --> 00:07:00,675
and the military and the
boats were the main employers.

108
00:07:00,675 --> 00:07:03,667
People worked for the garrison as tailors,

109
00:07:03,747 --> 00:07:07,550
shoemakers, laundrymen,
cooks and domestic servants.

110
00:07:07,550 --> 00:07:11,014
A tailor made suits for
British officers going home

111
00:07:11,094 --> 00:07:14,765
on leave and white shirts
for the Gurkhas, and a local

112
00:07:14,845 --> 00:07:18,250
photographer took the
army's ceremonial pictures.

113
00:07:18,250 --> 00:07:22,298
In the early 1960s, as more
ships anchored off Singapore,

114
00:07:22,378 --> 00:07:27,375
many men took up ferrying passengers
between the ships and the shore.

115
00:07:27,375 --> 00:07:31,415
One boatman's son recalled that
once his father had a boat,

116
00:07:31,495 --> 00:07:35,825
the family no longer worried
about money for Chinese New Year.

117
00:07:35,825 --> 00:07:39,549
The island still belonged to
the British War Department,

118
00:07:39,629 --> 00:07:42,550
and the villagers lived
there on its terms.

119
00:07:42,550 --> 00:07:47,622
Since 1945 they had travelled free
on the army ferries to Jardine Steps,

120
00:07:47,702 --> 00:07:51,988
but in June 1948 the army
announced that from the 1st of July

121
00:07:52,068 --> 00:07:56,711
the ferries would carry only service
personnel, their families and

122
00:07:56,791 --> 00:08:01,800
people employed by the services,
because it had fewer boats and crews.

123
00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:07,075
Everyone else had to pay 30
to 50 cents a trip by sampan.

124
00:08:07,075 --> 00:08:11,790
Islanders wrote to the newspapers
asking the army to be a good neighbour,

125
00:08:11,870 --> 00:08:14,483
and were reminded that people not working

126
00:08:14,563 --> 00:08:18,175
for it had no legal right to
live on the island at all.

127
00:08:18,175 --> 00:08:22,445
Until 1959 the villages drew
water from springs and wells,

128
00:08:22,525 --> 00:08:25,445
or bought it from the army's reservoirs,

129
00:08:25,525 --> 00:08:29,870
and in the 1950s, when the
wells ran short, the Rural Board

130
00:08:29,950 --> 00:08:33,775
paid the army to ship water
over from the mainland.

131
00:08:33,775 --> 00:08:37,523
That year the British Army
laid a 3.2-kilometre pipeline

132
00:08:37,603 --> 00:08:42,376
from Keppel Harbour, through Pulau
Brani, to a reservoir on the island,

133
00:08:42,456 --> 00:08:45,600
with free public standpipes
for the villagers.

134
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:50,707
Children went to an army primary school,
which took some kampong children,

135
00:08:50,787 --> 00:08:53,450
or to a small Chinese school, Sin Hwa.

136
00:08:53,450 --> 00:08:59,208
In 1964 the government built the
Blakang Mati Integrated Primary School,

137
00:08:59,288 --> 00:09:02,775
which taught in Malay,
English and Chinese.

138
00:09:02,775 --> 00:09:07,122
The island also had a mosque
near the ferry landing, a surau,

139
00:09:07,202 --> 00:09:11,775
a Chinese temple, a Hindu temple
and a chapel for the soldiers.

140
00:09:11,775 --> 00:09:16,632
From 1957 to 1969 its barracks
housed two battalions of Gurkhas,

141
00:09:16,712 --> 00:09:19,177
some of them with their families,

142
00:09:19,257 --> 00:09:23,500
who went to the island's
weddings and other gatherings.

143
00:09:23,500 --> 00:09:29,004
Under the agreement that brought
Singapore into Malaysia in 1963,

144
00:09:29,084 --> 00:09:32,697
Britain released 658
acres of Blakang Mati,

145
00:09:32,777 --> 00:09:38,275
and on the 1st of September 1967
the island passed to Singapore.

146
00:09:38,275 --> 00:09:41,634
The Singapore Armed Forces
took over the barracks,

147
00:09:41,714 --> 00:09:46,587
and the new government weighed several
uses for the island: a free port,

148
00:09:46,667 --> 00:09:50,450
a deep-water port, military
bases and a tourist resort.

149
00:09:50,450 --> 00:09:54,644
In 1967 the Minister for Law
and National Development,

150
00:09:54,724 --> 00:09:58,996
E. W. Barker, summed up the
competing claims and added,

151
00:09:59,076 --> 00:10:02,875
"I sincerely hope tourism
will not be left out."

152
00:10:02,875 --> 00:10:07,350
For a time an oil refinery
for Esso was planned there.

153
00:10:07,350 --> 00:10:11,139
The government's Dutch economic
adviser, Albert Winsemius,

154
00:10:11,219 --> 00:10:15,674
argued that a richer Singapore would
one day need space for leisure,

155
00:10:15,754 --> 00:10:19,410
and after he and the planner
Alan Choe put their case to

156
00:10:19,490 --> 00:10:23,425
the Prime Minister, Esso agreed
to build in Jurong instead.

157
00:10:23,425 --> 00:10:26,179
By late 1968 the government had

158
00:10:26,259 --> 00:10:29,550
decided to make the island a resort.

159
00:10:29,550 --> 00:10:34,209
A contest to rename it was
launched in November 1969,

160
00:10:34,289 --> 00:10:38,859
and from September 1970
Blakang Mati became Sentosa,

161
00:10:38,939 --> 00:10:41,175
Malay for "tranquillity".

162
00:10:41,175 --> 00:10:45,791
The Sentosa Development
Corporation was set up in 1972,

163
00:10:45,871 --> 00:10:48,780
and the cable car from Mount Faber,

164
00:10:48,860 --> 00:10:53,300
its first major attraction,
opened in February 1974.

165
00:10:53,300 --> 00:10:57,900
In the early 1970s the villagers
were moved to the mainland,

166
00:10:57,980 --> 00:11:03,675
many of them to new Housing and
Development Board flats at Telok Blangah.

167
00:11:03,675 --> 00:11:07,874
One former resident remembered
that many people were sad to leave,

168
00:11:07,954 --> 00:11:10,726
afraid that the community
would be split up,

169
00:11:10,806 --> 00:11:15,150
and that she still went back to
stay a day or two at her old house.

170
00:11:15,150 --> 00:11:18,830
Some islanders found jobs
at the new attractions,

171
00:11:18,910 --> 00:11:23,975
and the integrated school's site
became Sentosa's Maritime Museum.

172
00:11:23,975 --> 00:11:26,543
Sitting apart from the main island,

173
00:11:26,623 --> 00:11:29,800
Sentosa was chosen for some unusual tasks.

174
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:33,912
In 1971 Singapore's first
satellite earth station was built

175
00:11:33,992 --> 00:11:37,962
on the slopes of Mount Serapong,
away from buildings that

176
00:11:38,042 --> 00:11:42,082
could interfere with its
signals, and on the 8th of August

177
00:11:42,162 --> 00:11:46,416
that year it brought in Singapore's
first television pictures

178
00:11:46,496 --> 00:11:50,475
by satellite from overseas,
of the Apollo 15 splashdown.

179
00:11:50,475 --> 00:11:56,522
In May 1989 Chia Thye Poh, a former
Barisan Sosialis member of parliament,

180
00:11:56,602 --> 00:11:59,834
was released after more than 22 years of

181
00:11:59,914 --> 00:12:04,550
detention without trial under
the Internal Security Act.

182
00:12:04,550 --> 00:12:07,253
The government said he
had been a member of

183
00:12:07,333 --> 00:12:10,375
the Communist Party of
Malaya, which he denied.

184
00:12:10,375 --> 00:12:14,298
He was required to live on
Sentosa, in a reconstructed

185
00:12:14,378 --> 00:12:19,043
guardhouse at Fort Siloso, and
later worked there as a freelance

186
00:12:19,123 --> 00:12:22,824
translator for the Sentosa
Development Corporation,

187
00:12:22,904 --> 00:12:27,500
until he was allowed to move to
the mainland in November 1992.

188
00:12:27,500 --> 00:12:31,219
On the 12th of June 2018
the island hosted the first

189
00:12:31,299 --> 00:12:34,873
meeting between a serving
United States president,

190
00:12:34,953 --> 00:12:38,825
Donald Trump, and a North
Korean leader, Kim Jong Un.

191
00:12:38,825 --> 00:12:42,468
They met at the Capella
hotel, whose front is formed

192
00:12:42,548 --> 00:12:46,200
by the restored Officers'
Mess of the old garrison.

193
00:12:46,200 --> 00:12:49,764
Among the police who secured
it were members of the

194
00:12:49,844 --> 00:12:53,194
Gurkha Contingent of the
Singapore Police Force,

195
00:12:53,274 --> 00:12:57,775
half a century after the Gurkha
battalions had left the island.

196
00:12:57,775 --> 00:13:02,441
Fort Siloso is now a museum of
the island's military history,

197
00:13:02,521 --> 00:13:05,241
and in 2022 eleven of its structures

198
00:13:05,321 --> 00:13:08,900
were gazetted together
as a National Monument.

199
00:13:08,900 --> 00:13:12,975
The barracks around the old
parade ground were conserved

200
00:13:13,055 --> 00:13:16,982
in 2004 and turned into
hotels, restaurants and a spa,

201
00:13:17,062 --> 00:13:20,550
and the parade ground is
now the Barracks Lawn.

202
00:13:20,550 --> 00:13:25,235
The Officers' Mess, conserved
in 2000, is part of the Capella,

203
00:13:25,315 --> 00:13:30,925
and the old military hospital became
the waxworks museum Madame Tussauds.

204
00:13:30,925 --> 00:13:35,329
Of the kampongs nothing is
left: the Hokkien village by the

205
00:13:35,409 --> 00:13:40,344
jetty lies under Universal Studios,
part of Resorts World Sentosa,

206
00:13:40,424 --> 00:13:44,300
which opened in 2010 with
Singapore's first casino.

207
00:13:44,300 --> 00:13:48,083
The Yeo clan association
set up in the 1960s still

208
00:13:48,163 --> 00:13:51,482
brings former islanders
together for dinners

209
00:13:51,562 --> 00:13:55,425
and festivals in honour
of the deity Tua Pek Kong.

210
00:13:55,425 --> 00:13:59,614
Why it matters: Sentosa is one of
Singapore's most visited places,

211
00:13:59,694 --> 00:14:02,848
but few of the people who
go there for its beaches

212
00:14:02,928 --> 00:14:07,571
and theme parks know that it was a
home for fishing families and boatmen,

213
00:14:07,651 --> 00:14:10,287
a garrison for British and Indian gunners,

214
00:14:10,367 --> 00:14:13,925
a prisoner-of-war camp and
a place of wartime killings.

215
00:14:13,925 --> 00:14:19,023
The island's conserved forts and
barracks keep the military story visible,

216
00:14:19,103 --> 00:14:23,012
while the kampong story survives
mainly in oral histories

217
00:14:23,092 --> 00:14:27,500
and in the clan association its
former residents still keep up.

218
00:14:27,500 --> 00:14:31,036
Next time you take the cable
car across, look for the

219
00:14:31,116 --> 00:14:34,174
red roofs of the old
barracks among the trees,

220
00:14:34,254 --> 00:14:38,075
and remember the villages
that stood by the jetty below.
